<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<jmeterTestPlan version="1.2" properties="5.0" jmeter="5.6.3">
  <hashTree>
    <TestPlan guiclass="TestPlanGui" testclass="TestPlan"
              testname="Case Management — Re-accreditation Performance Tests" enabled="true">
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Performance test — re-accreditation work-item lifecycle journeys.
Pre-seed work items with: jmeter/scripts/seed-cms-work-items.sh

Rewritten for app template v12 (RA-410 removed the task-checklist model and the
withdraw-from-case-management journey; RA-291/RA-337 replaced it with duly-make
-> self-assign -> log-decision, plus a standalone query endpoint). See
jmeter/scripts/seed-cms-work-items.sh and case-management-frontend's
src/server/work-items/re-accreditation/module.js for the authoritative flow.

Load profile: 100 pre-seeded work items, ramping 2 -> 10 concurrent users over 16s.
Each of the 3 thread groups starts its first thread at t=0 (3 threads active
immediately — the "starts at 2" end of the ramp) and stages the rest of its
threads evenly across ThreadGroup.ramp_time=16s, so combined concurrency climbs
from ~3 workers to the full 10 by t=16s and then holds at 10 while the queues
drain (stock JMeter has no built-in "start at N, climb to M" thread group, so
this staggered-start-per-group approach is the standard idiom for a visible
ramp rather than an instant burst to peak concurrency).

Each thread group is a worker pool draining its own CSV as a shared queue —
CSVDataSet has recycle=false/stopThread=true, and the Loop Controller loops each
thread forever (continue_forever=true) until its group's CSV is exhausted, so
every row is processed exactly once and threads finish as soon as the queue is
empty (not all at the same wall-clock time, since group sizes/thread counts
differ). on_sample_error=continue so one item's failure doesn't stop a worker
from picking up the next queued item.

To change the load profile:
  1. Work-item counts (must sum to the total you want): regenerate CSVs with
     APPROVE_COUNT=.. REFUSE_COUNT=.. QUERY_COUNT=.. ./jmeter/scripts/seed-cms-work-items.sh
  2. Concurrent users / ramp: edit ThreadGroup.num_threads and .ramp_time on
     each of the 3 Thread Groups below (num_threads must sum to the total users
     you want, and each group's thread count should not exceed that group's
     own CSV row count, or the excess threads just stop immediately on an
     empty queue).

Thread Groups (ramping 2->10 users total over 16s):
  1. Approval  (4 threads) draining cms-approve.csv (43 rows)
     duly-make -> self-assign (auto: duly-made -> assessment-in-progress) -> decision=approved
  2. Refusal   (3 threads) draining cms-refuse.csv (29 rows)
     duly-make -> self-assign -> decision=rejected
  3. Query     (3 threads) draining cms-query.csv (28 rows)
     query (submitted -> queried) via the query-during-duly-making transition
Total: 100 pre-seeded work items, 10 workers at peak (4+3+3), ramped from 2-3.

KNOWN LOCAL LIMITATION: the "Log decision" POST (final step of Approval/Refusal)
pushes a decision-status-changed notification to the operator-facing backend
(epr-register-enrol-backend). Work items seeded directly into MongoDB — including
everything jmeter/scripts/seed-cms-work-items.sh produces — have no matching
record there, so that push 404s and case-management-backend ABANDONS the decision
server-side ("No state was changed" in its logs) and returns a plain error-banner
redirect. The frontend still responds 302 -> 200 on that redirect, so THIS SAMPLER
SHOWS AS PASSING even though the work item never left assessment-in-progress —
verified directly against MongoDB after a local run. Duly-make, self-assign and
query are unaffected and genuinely complete (verified: submitted -> duly-made,
duly-made -> assessment-in-progress, submitted -> queried). Reaching a real
Approved/Rejected end state requires the work item to originate from a genuine
submission through the operator-facing app (epr-register-enrol-frontend), which
is out of scope for this plan — do not read a green run of this plan as proof
that approval/refusal actually completed locally.</stringProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.functional_mode">false</boolProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.tearDown_on_shutdown">false</boolProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.serialize_threadgroups">false</boolProp>
